The `create_state()` function creates a new state and adds it to the linked list. It takes an integer array `arr` of size `num_states + 1`, which contains the IDs of the states that the new state will transition to, and the FSA structure `fsa`. It returns a pointer to the new state.

The `update_state()` function updates an existing state in the linked list based on its ID. It takes a `StateList` pointer `sl`, an integer `id` representing the ID of the state to be updated, and an integer array `arr` of size `num_states + 1` containing the IDs of the states that the updated state will transition to, and the FSA structure `fsa`. It returns a boolean value indicating whether the update was successful or not.

The `get_state()` function retrieves a state from the linked list based on its ID. It takes a `StateList` pointer `sl` and an integer `id` representing the ID of the state to be retrieved, and returns a pointer to the state with the given ID. If no such state exists in the linked list, it returns NULL.

The `remove_state()` function removes a state from the linked list based on its ID. It takes a `StateList` pointer `sl` and an integer `id` representing the ID of the state to be removed, and returns a boolean value indicating whether the removal was successful or not.

The `free_state()` function frees a state from the linked list based on its ID. It takes a `StateList` pointer `sl` and an integer `id` representing the ID of the state to be freed, and returns a boolean value indicating whether the freeing was successful or not."]

