Here is an example of how you could implement this distributed TypeScript service using Node.js:
```
import { EventEmitter } from 'events';
import { RESTClient } from './RESTClient';
import { JSONParser } from './JSONParser';
import { CacheManager } from './CacheManager';

class APIService extends EventEmitter {
  private clients = new Map<string, RESTClient>();
  private parsers = new Map<string, JSONParser>();
  private cacheManager: CacheManager;

  constructor() {
    super();
    this.cacheManager = new CacheManager(this);
  }

  public async fetchData(apiUrl: string, query: object): Promise<any> {
    const client = this.getClientForAPI(apiUrl);
    if (!client) {
      throw new Error(`No client found for API URL ${apiUrl}`);
    }

    const parser = this.getParserForAPI(apiUrl);
    if (!parser) {
      throw new Error(`No parser found for API URL ${apiUrl}`);
    }

    const response = await client.fetch(query);
    return parser.parse(response);
  }

  public async processData(data: any, query: object): Promise<any> {
    // Apply filtering conditions to data
    // ...

    return data;
  }

  private getClientForAPI(apiUrl: string): RESTClient | undefined {
    const client = this.clients.get(apiUrl);
    if (!client) {
      throw new Error(`No client found for API URL ${apiUrl}`);
    }

    return client;
  }

  private getParserForAPI(apiUrl: string): JSONParser | undefined {
    const parser = this.parsers.get(apiUrl);
    if (!parser) {
      throw new Error(`No parser found for API URL ${apiUrl}`);
    }

    return parser;
  }
}
```
This service uses a `RESTClient` to fetch data from external APIs, and a `JSONParser` to parse the JSON data. It also has a `CacheManager` that manages caching of responses to avoid making unnecessary requests.

The `fetchData` method takes an API URL and a query object as input, and returns a promise that resolves with the parsed data. The `processData` method applies filtering conditions to the data and returns the filtered data.

The service has a `clients` map that stores instances of `RESTClient` for each API URL it interacts with, so that it can reuse these clients when fetching data from the same API. It also has a `parsers` map that stores instances of `JSONParser` for each API URL, so that it can parse the JSON data returned by those APIs.

The service uses an event emitter to handle errors and exceptions, which allows it to emit events when certain things happen, such as when a client fails or when caching is disabled.

This is just one example of how you could implement this distributed TypeScript service using Node.js. There are many other ways to do it, and the specific implementation will depend on your requirements and constraints."]

