The `create_cryptographic_library()` function is used to create an instance of the library, which returns a pointer to a new `cryptographic_library` struct that contains pointers to the four sets of functions. The `destroy_cryptographic_library()` function is used to free resources allocated by the library when it is no longer needed.

In this example, we have defined a `plugin_registry` struct that contains a pointer to a linked list of plugins. Each plugin has a unique name and a pointer to a set of functions that provide its functionality. The `register_plugin()` function adds a new plugin to the registry, while the `unregister_plugin()` function removes an existing plugin from the registry.

The `resource_manager` struct contains a pointer to a linked list of resources that are used by plugins. Each resource has a unique name and a value that represents its current state. The `acquire_resource()` function is used to acquire a new resource, while the `release_resource()` function is used to release an existing resource when it is no longer needed.

The `hash_functions` struct contains a pointer to a linked list of hash algorithms and their respective functions. Each algorithm has a unique name and a set of functions that provide its functionality. The `create_hash()` function creates a new hash context, while the `update_hash()` function updates an existing hash context with new data. Finally, the `finalize_hash()` function finalizes the hash calculation and returns the resulting hash value.

The `block_ciphers` struct contains a pointer to a linked list of block cipher algorithms and their respective functions. Each algorithm has a unique name and a set of functions that provide its functionality. The `create_block_cipher()` function creates a new block cipher context, while the `encrypt_block_cipher()` function encrypts data using an existing block cipher context, and finally the `decrypt_block_cipher()` function decrypts data using an existing block cipher context.

