Here is an example implementation of the requested authentication token management system:
```scss
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <time.h>
#include <math.h>
#include <stdbool.h>

// Define the data structure for storing token information
struct Token {
    char* token;
    int expirationTime;
    bool isProduction;
};

// Define the interface for the timestamp and book reference service
typedef struct TimestampService {
    time_t getCurrentTimestamp();
} TimestampService;

// Define the function for appending a timestamp with book references
static void appendTimestampWithBookReferences(const char* str, int sizeLimit, bool isProduction, TimestampService* timestampService) {
    // Check if the input string is empty
    if (str == NULL || *str == '\0') {
        return;
    }

    // Get the current timestamp from the timestamp service
    time_t currentTimestamp = timestampService->getCurrentTimestamp();

    // Calculate the expiration time for the token
    int expirationTime = (int)ceil(currentTimestamp / 1000 + 3600);

    // Create a new token structure with the calculated expiration time
    struct Token* token = malloc(sizeof(struct Token));
    token->token = str;
    token->expirationTime = expirationTime;
    token->isProduction = isProduction;

    // Append the token to the input string
    char* outputString = malloc(strlen(str) + 10);
    sprintf(outputString, "%s-%d", str, expirationTime);

    // Free the memory allocated for the input string
    free((void*)str);

    // Return the appended string
    return outputString;
}

// Define the main function for testing the token management system
int main() {
    // Create a new timestamp service instance
    TimestampService* timestampService = malloc(sizeof(TimestampService));

    // Set up the timestamp service with the current time
    timestampService->getCurrentTimestamp();

    // Define the input string for testing
    const char* inputString = "token-1234";

    // Test the token management system with a non-production environment
    bool isProduction = false;
    struct Token* token = appendTimestampWithBookReferences(inputString, 100, isProduction, timestampService);
    printf("Token: %s\n", token->token);
    free(token);

    // Test the token management system with a production environment
    isProduction = true;
    token = appendTimestampWithBookReferences(inputString, 100, isProduction, timestampService);
    printf("Token: %s\n", token->token);
    free(token);

    // Free the memory allocated for the timestamp service
    free((void*)timestampService);

    return 0;
}
```
This implementation defines a `struct Token` data structure to store information about each token, including the token string itself, its expiration time, and a flag indicating whether it is a production environment or not. It also defines an interface for the timestamp and book reference service, which is used to calculate the expiration time of each token based on the current timestamp.

The `appendTimestampWithBookReferences` function takes in a pointer to a constant char array (`const char* str`), a maximum length `sizeLimit`, a boolean `isProduction`, and a `timestampService` instance. It creates a new token structure with the calculated expiration time based on the current timestamp and the production environment flag, and then appends the token string to the input string. The function also frees the memory allocated for the input string.

The main function is used for testing the token management system with different input strings and production environments. It creates a new `TimestampService` instance and sets up the timestamp service with the current time. Then, it tests the token management system with a non-production environment and a production environment by passing in different input strings and setting the `isProduction` flag accordingly. Finally, the function frees the memory allocated for the timestamp service instance.']

