Here is an example of how you can implement the modular, extensible C++ library for managing a large-scale event log system in a multi-cloud, distributed infrastructure environment:
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>
#include <utility>
#include <memory>
#include <chrono>

// Forward declarations
class LogEntry;
class LogStorage;
class LogRetentionPolicy;
class LogArchive;
class LogForwarder;
class LogConsumer;

// Define the main class for the event log system
class EventLog {
public:
    // Constructor to set up the log system
    EventLog(std::string name, std::vector<std::shared_ptr<LogStorage>> storage_list, std::shared_ptr<LogRetentionPolicy> retention_policy, std::shared_ptr<LogArchive> archive, std::shared_ptr<LogForwarder> forwarder);

    // Method to add a log entry to the system
    void AddEntry(std::string entry);

    // Method to get the current number of log entries in the system
    int GetNumEntries() const;

private:
    std::string name_;                          // Name of the event log system
    std::vector<std::shared_ptr<LogStorage>> storage_list_;  // List of log storages
    std::shared_ptr<LogRetentionPolicy> retention_policy_;   // Retention policy for logs
    std::shared_ptr<LogArchive> archive_;                  // Log archive
    std::shared_ptr<LogForwarder> forwarder_;             // Log forwarder
};

// Define the log storage interface
class LogStorage {
public:
    virtual void AddEntry(std::string entry) = 0;
    virtual int GetNumEntries() const = 0;
};

// Define a class for each type of log storage (e.g., in-memory, file-based, cloud-based)
class InMemoryLogStorage : public LogStorage {
public:
    void AddEntry(std::string entry);
    int GetNumEntries() const;

private:
    std::vector<std::shared_ptr<LogEntry>> entries_;  // List of log entries
};

class FileBasedLogStorage : public LogStorage {
public:
    void AddEntry(std::string entry);
    int GetNumEntries() const;

private:
    std::vector<std::shared_ptr<LogEntry>> entries_;  // List of log entries
};

class CloudBasedLogStorage : public LogStorage {
public:
    void AddEntry(std::string entry);
    int GetNumEntries() const;

private:
    std::vector<std::shared_ptr<LogEntry>> entries_;  // List of log entries
};

// Define the log retention policy interface
class LogRetentionPolicy {
public:
    virtual bool ShouldKeep(std::string entry) = 0;
};

// Define a class for each type of log retention policy (e.g., time-based, size-based)
class TimeBasedLogRetentionPolicy : public LogRetentionPolicy {
public:
    bool ShouldKeep(std::string entry);
};

class SizeBasedLogRetentionPolicy : public LogRetentionPolicy {
public:
    bool ShouldKeep(std::string entry);
};

// Define the log archive interface
class LogArchive {
public:
    virtual void Archive(std::string entry) = 0;
};

// Define a class for each type of log archive (e.g., cold storage, warm storage)
class ColdStorageLogArchive : public LogArchive {
public:
    void Archive(std::string entry);
};

class WarmStorageLogArchive : public LogArchive {
public:
    void Archive(std::string entry);
};

// Define the log forwarder interface
class LogForwarder {
public:
    virtual void Forward(std::string entry) = 0;
};

// Define a class for each type of log forwarder (e.g., REST API, CLI)
class RestApiLogForwarder : public LogForwarder {
public:
    void Forward(std::string entry);
};

class CliLogForwarder : public LogForwarder {
public:
    void Forward(std::string entry);
};

// Define the log consumer interface
class LogConsumer {
public:
    virtual void Consume(std::string entry) = 0;
};

// Define a class for each type of log consumer (e.g., email, slack, splunk)
class EmailLogConsumer : public LogConsumer {
public:
    void Consume(std::string entry);
};

class SlackLogConsumer : public LogConsumer {
public:
    void Consume(std::string entry);
};

class SplunkLogConsumer : public LogConsumer {
public:
    void Consume(std::string entry);
};
```
This code defines the main `EventLog` class that handles the log system, and several supporting classes for each type of storage, retention policy, archive, forwarder, and consumer. The main class has a constructor to set up the log system with the given parameters, and methods to add a log entry and get the current number of log entries.

The `LogStorage` interface defines the common functionality for all types of log storages, while each type of storage (e.g., in-memory, file-based, cloud-based) implements the `AddEntry` method and the `GetNumEntries` method. The `LogRetentionPolicy` interface defines the common functionality for all types of retention policies, while each type of policy (e.g., time-based, size-based) implements the `ShouldKeep` method.

The `LogArchive` interface defines the common functionality for all types of log archives, while each type of archive (e.g., cold storage, warm storage) implements the `Archive` method. The `LogForwarder` interface defines the common functionality for all types of log forwarders, while each type of forwarder (e.g., REST API, CLI) implements the `Forward` method.

The `LogConsumer` interface defines the common functionality for all types of log consumers, while each type of consumer (e.g., email, slack, splunk) implements the `Consume` method.

This code provides a modular and extensible architecture for managing logs with different storage options, retention policies, archives, forwarders, and consumers. It also allows for easy addition of new types of log storages, retention policies, archives, forwarders, and consumers.']

