Here is an example of a Java method that establishes a connection to a remote address using a Java Channel:
```
import java.net.InetSocketAddress;
import java.nio.channels.AsynchronousSocketChannel;
import java.util.concurrent.CountDownLatch;

public class AsynchronousSocketChannelExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        // Create a new asynchronous socket channel
        AsynchronousSocketChannel channel = AsynchronousSocketChannel.open();

        // Connect to the remote address
        InetSocketAddress remoteAddress = new InetSocketAddress("www.example.com", 80);
        channel.connect(remoteAddress).get();

        // Handle connection state changes
        CountDownLatch latch = new CountDownLatch(1);
        channel.setInterestOps(SelectionKey.OP_READ);
        channel.register(latch, SelectionKey.OP_CONNECT);

        // Wait for the connection to be established
        while (!latch.await(100, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS)) {
            if (channel.isConnectionPending()) {
                channel.finishConnect();
            } else {
                break;
            }
        }
    }
}
```
This method creates a new asynchronous socket channel using the `AsynchronousSocketChannel.open()` method, and then connects to the remote address using the `connect()` method. It also sets up an interest operation for connection state changes using the `setInterestOps()` method and registers the channel with a latch object to wait for the connection to be established.

The method uses a while loop to wait for the connection to be established, checking for any pending connections using the `isConnectionPending()` method. If there is a pending connection, it finishes the connection using the `finishConnect()` method. The method then breaks out of the loop once the connection has been established or a timeout occurs.

This method can be used to establish a connection to a remote address asynchronously using Java NIO channels. It handles various connection states and provides a way to wait for the connection to be established before proceeding with other tasks.']

