  }
  // Process the buffer asynchronously using a thread pool
  for (int i = 0; i < num_threads; i++) {
    pthread_create(&thread[i], NULL, process_buffer, buffer + (i * chunk_size));
  }
  // Wait for all threads to finish
  for (int i = 0; i < num_threads; i++) {
    pthread_join(thread[i], NULL);
  }
  // Close the memory-mapped file
  munmap(buffer, file_size);
}
```

This code is more complex than the previous one, but it demonstrates how to use asynchronous I/O and memory-mapped files to process large input and output files. The `process_async_file()` function uses a thread pool to asynchronously read chunks of the file into memory, and the `process_buffer()` function processes each chunk as needed.

Note that this code requires the `pthread` library to be installed on your system, which provides a way for multiple threads to run concurrently within a single process. If you don't have `pthread`, you can use other concurrency primitives such as mutexes and condition variables instead of thread pools.']

