```c++
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <string>
#include <fstream>
#include <sstream>
#include <iomanip>

using namespace std;

// Define a struct to represent the resources allocated to the application
struct Resource {
  string type; // Type of resource (e.g., database connection, background thread)
  int usage; // Usage of the resource (e.g., number of active connections)
};

// Define a vector to store the resources allocated to the application
vector<Resource> resources;

// Define a function to load the resources from a file
void loadResources(const string& filename) {
  ifstream input_file(filename);
  string line;
  while (getline(input_file, line)) {
    // Split the line into tokens using a delimiter of space
    vector<string> tokens = split(line, ' ');
    // Add each resource to the resources vector
    Resource r;
    r.type = tokens[0];
    r.usage = stoi(tokens[1]);
    resources.push_back(r);
  }
}

// Define a function to serialize the resources to a file
void saveResources(const string& filename) {
  ofstream output_file(filename);
  for (auto r : resources) {
    // Serialize each resource as a line in the file
    output_file << r.type << " " << r.usage << endl;
  }
}

// Define a function to handle deserialization failures
void handleDeserializationFailure(const string& error) {
  // Throw a RuntimeException with detailed error information
  throw runtime_error("Failed to deserialize resource: " + error);
}

int main() {
  // Load the resources from a file
  loadResources("resources.txt");

  // Use the resources in the application
  for (auto r : resources) {
    // Do something with each resource
    cout << r.type << " usage: " << r.usage << endl;
  }

  // Serialize the resources to a file
  saveResources("resources.txt");

  return 0;
}
```
In this example, we have defined a struct `Resource` to represent the resources allocated to the application, and a vector `resources` to store the resources. We have also defined functions `loadResources` and `saveResources` to load and serialize the resources from/to a file, respectively. In addition, we have defined a function `handleDeserializationFailure` to handle deserialization failures.

In the main function, we first load the resources from a file using `loadResources`. Then, we use each resource in the application by iterating over the vector of resources and performing some operation on each one (in this example, we just print out the type and usage of each resource). Finally, we serialize the resources to a file using `saveResources`.

Note that this is just an example implementation and may not be suitable for your specific use case. You may need to modify or replace certain components based on your requirements.']

