  mysqlpp::Connection conn("localhost", "username", "password");

  if (!conn.connect()) {
    cout << "Database connection failed." << endl;
    return;
  }

  mysqlpp::Query q(query);
  q % new_balance;
  q % account_id;

  try {
    mysqlpp::StoreQueryResult res = q.execute();
    if (res.rows() == 0) {
      cout << "No rows affected." << endl;
    } else {
      cout << "Account balance updated successfully." << endl;
    }
  } catch (const std::exception &e) {
    cerr << "Error updating account balance: " << e.what() << endl;
  }
}
```
This function takes an account ID and a new balance as input parameters, and updates the corresponding account balance in the `Accounts` table. It uses a parameterized query to prevent SQL injection, and connects to the database using the specified username and password. If the connection fails or no rows are affected (i.e., the account ID is not found), the function prints an appropriate error message. Otherwise, it prints a success message.

Note that this code assumes you have already set up a MySQL database with a `Accounts` table containing columns for `account_id`, `account_balance`, and `currency`. You will need to modify the connection parameters (host name, username, password) to match your specific database setup.']

