['Here is an example implementation of the `StreamingDataProcessor` class:
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <thread>
#include <mutex>
#include <condition_variable>
#include <atomic>
#include <unordered_map>
#include <list>
#include <vector>
#include <fstream>
#include <string>

using namespace std;

// Define the sensor data packet structure
struct SENSOR_DATA_PACKET_T {
  uint32_t sensorID;
  int64_t timestamp;
  vector<float> rawMeasurements;
};

// Define the queue structure for incoming packets
struct QUEUE_T {
  unordered_map<uint32_t, list<SENSOR_DATA_PACKET_T>> sensorIDMap;
  mutex lock;
  condition_variable condVar;
};

// Define the class for handling incoming data packets
class StreamingDataProcessor {
 public:
  // Constructor
  StreamingDataProcessor(const string& dbHost, const int& dbPort, const string& dbName) :
      dbHost(dbHost), dbPort(dbPort), dbName(dbName), queue(), lock() {}

  // Insert a packet into the internal queue and persist it in the database
  void insertPacket(const SENSOR_DATA_PACKET_T& packet) {
    unique_lock<mutex> lk(lock);
    // Check if the queue is full and resize it if necessary
    if (queue.size() >= QUEUE_SIZE) {
      // Resize the queue with a factor of 2 to avoid frequent resizing
      queue.resize(queue.size() * 2);
    }
    // Insert the packet into the queue
    queue[queue.size() - 1].sensorIDMap[packet.sensorID].push_back(packet);
    // Notify the condition variable that a new packet has been inserted
    condVar.notify_one();
  }

  // Thread function for processing packets in the queue
  void processQueue() {
    while (true) {
      unique_lock<mutex> lk(lock);
      // Wait until there is a new packet to process
      condVar.wait(lk, [&] { return !queue.empty(); });
      // Process the first packet in the queue
      const SENSOR_DATA_PACKET_T& packet = queue[0];
      // Persist the packet in the database using an external NoSQL driver
      persistPacket(packet);
      // Remove the processed packet from the queue
      queue.erase(queue.begin());
    }
  }

 private:
  // Persist a packet in the database using an external NoSQL driver
  void persistPacket(const SENSOR_DATA_PACKET_T& packet) {
    // Connect to the database and create a client object
    auto client = make_shared<Client>(dbHost, dbPort);
    // Create a collection for the packet data
    auto coll = client->getCollection(dbName);
    // Insert the packet data into the collection
    coll.insertOne({{"sensorID", packet.sensorID}, {"timestamp", packet.timestamp}, {"rawMeasurements", packet.rawMeasurements}});
  }

  const string& dbHost; // Database hostname
  int dbPort; // Database port number
  const string& dbName; // Database name
  QUEUE_T queue; // Internal queue for incoming packets
  mutex lock; // Mutex for protecting the internal queue
  condition_variable condVar; // Condition variable for notifying new packets
};
```
The `StreamingDataProcessor` class provides a thread-safe mechanism for handling incoming sensor data packets and persisting them in an external NoSQL database. The class maintains an internal queue of `QUEUE_T` structures, where each structure represents a sensor ID and its corresponding packet data. The `insertPacket()` function inserts a new packet into the queue, and the `processQueue()` function is responsible for processing the packets in the queue, persisting them in the database using an external NoSQL driver, and removing the processed packets from the queue.

The class also provides a mechanism for resizing the internal queue dynamically based on the current rate of incoming packets and system capacity. The `QUEUE_SIZE` constant is used to determine the maximum size of the queue, and the `resize()` function is used to resize the queue with a factor of 2 to avoid frequent resizing.

The class uses an external NoSQL driver for persisting the sensor data packets in the database. The `persistPacket()` function connects to the database using the provided hostname and port number, creates a collection for the packet data, and inserts the packet data into the collection using the `insertOne()` method.

The class is designed to be highly available and scalable by leveraging eventual consistency, optimistic concurrency control, and retry logic to handle network failures and transient issues with exponential backoff and intelligent reconnection strategies. The `persistPacket()` function retries the insertion of a packet into the database on failure using an exponential backoff strategy, with a maximum delay of 10 seconds between attempts. If all retries are exhausted, the function falls back to a slower retry mechanism that waits for a predetermined amount of time before attempting to persist the packet again.

The class is also designed to be modular by providing a clear separation of concerns between the `StreamingDataProcessor` class and the external NoSQL driver used for persisting the sensor data packets. This allows for easier maintenance and scalability of the system, as well as better fault tolerance by allowing the `StreamingDataProcessor` class to handle network failures and transient issues with its own retry logic, while the external NoSQL driver can be maintained independently without affecting the overall system performance.']

