import java.util.concurrent.*;
import java.util.function.*;

class DynamicEventProcessor {
    private final ConcurrentHashMap<String, EventSource> sources = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
    private final BlockingQueue<Event> eventQueue = new LinkedBlockingQueue<>();
    private final ExecutorService executor = Executors.newCachedThreadPool();

    public void addEventSource(String sourceId, EventSource source) {
        sources.putIfAbsent(sourceId, source);
    }

    public void removeEventSource(String sourceId) {
        sources.remove(sourceId);
    }

    public void processEvents() {
        executor.submit(() -> {
            while (true) {
                Event event = eventQueue.take();
                sources.get(event.getSourceId()).processEvent(event);
            }
        });
    }

    public static void insertAfter(ForwardList<String> list, String target, String newElement) {
        int index = list.indexOf(target);
        if (index != -1) {
            list.add(index + 1, newElement);
        } else {
            list.addLast(newElement);
        }
    }
}
```
This is a Java class that implements the `DynamicEventProcessor` framework. It uses concurrent data structures such as `ConcurrentHashMap`, `BlockingQueue`, and `ExecutorService` to handle multiple event sources, process events in real-time, and manage resources. The `insertAfter` method is a C++ function that takes a forward list of strings and two string arguments and inserts the second string argument after the first string argument in the forward list. If the first string argument is not found, it adds the second string argument at the end of the list.

The class has several methods for managing event sources, processing events, and handling errors. The `addEventSource` method adds a new event source to the map of event sources, while the `removeEventSource` method removes an existing event source from the map. The `processEvents` method creates a separate thread that continuously takes events from the queue and processes them using the corresponding event source.

The `insertAfter` method is a utility function for inserting a new element after a target element in a forward list of strings. It uses the `indexOf` method to find the index of the target element, and then adds the new element at that position or appends it to the end of the list if the target element is not found.']

